USING KEY is a novel approach to recursive common table expressions in SQL, extending the semantics of the classic WITH RECURSIVE clause to solve many of its issues. Due to its novelty, there is a lack of queries actually utilizing this approach. Previous implementations lack thorough comparison of code and performance between the two variants. This thesis presents four well-known algorithms in both variants to compare their implementation, runtime and memory performance. Three algorithms benefit from USING KEY on all counts, while one falls behind its classic counterpart.